-- Passage rg10-comparing-texts-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('43b4f611-d8d6-5d1f-8121-33b8d77305c2', 'rg10-comparing-texts-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Paths for Strangers$q$, $q$Text 1: How the Spider Gave the Stars Their Paths (an invented myth)

Long ago, in a story told by an imagined desert people, the stars wandered the sky like scattered sand, and travelers who crossed the dunes at night lost their way. The spider Ilu felt sorry for them. Each night she climbed the tallest rock and spun silver threads from one star to the next, tying them into shapes a walker could recognize: a fishhook, a bowl, a pair of running hares.

The work was slow, and the wind tore her threads again and again. The other animals mocked her, asking why a creature so small would labor for travelers she would never meet. Ilu answered that a path is a gift you give to strangers. When at last the patterns held, the travelers looked up, named the shapes and walked home safely. The threads have faded from sight, the myth says, but the paths remain, which is why the stars still form pictures that guide those who know how to read them.

Text 2: The Night Bus Map (a story)

When Dashiell Ferreira started his summer internship at the city transit office, he expected to make photocopies. Instead his supervisor, Ms. Hanna Lindqvist, handed him a problem: the night bus map was so cluttered that riders kept boarding the wrong lines after midnight. Complaints arrived every week from nurses, bakers and cleaning crews who traveled while the rest of the city slept.

Dashiell spent weeks redrawing it. He removed the daytime routes, gave each night line its own bright color and marked the transfer points with simple symbols: a moon, a clock, a pair of keys. His first versions were rejected for being too crowded, and he started over more times than he could count. A coworker asked why he cared so much about riders he would never meet.

He did not have a good answer then. But on the last day of his internship, waiting for a late bus himself, he watched a woman in hospital scrubs study the new map at the shelter, trace a line with her finger and step confidently onto the right bus. Dashiell realized that a good map works like a quiet favor. Its maker disappears, and the people who use it simply find their way home.$q$, 'draft')
