-- Passage rg10-comparing-texts-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('90729897-be3b-5689-bf8d-81e5948386b6', 'rg10-comparing-texts-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Three Tasks$q$, $q$Text 1: The Three Tasks of the Well Keeper (an invented folktale pattern)

In an invented folktale about a hill village, an old well keeper named Sefa needed an apprentice. Three young people asked for the job, and she gave each one the same three tasks. First, carry water from the spring without spilling. Second, find out why the east well tasted of iron. Third, teach a child how to lower a bucket.

The first applicant ran so fast that half the water splashed away. The second guessed that the iron came from a buried nail and refused to dig further. The third, a quiet girl named Ama, walked slowly with the water, asked the farmers which fields drained toward the east well and spent an afternoon letting a small boy practice with the rope until he could do it alone. Sefa chose Ama. "Speed fills no well," she said, "and a keeper who cannot teach keeps nothing for long."

Text 2: Three Samples (a story)

Ines Vukovic's internship at the county water lab began with a test she did not know she was taking. On the first morning, Dr. Raymond Osei gave her and two other interns three assignments: carry a tray of water samples from the field van to the lab, figure out why the samples from Marsh Road showed high iron, and show a visiting middle schooler how to use a pipette.

One intern hurried across the parking lot and cracked a vial. Another wrote "old pipes" in his notebook for Marsh Road and moved on to lunch. Ines carried the tray slowly. She searched the county records and found an old map showing a former ironworks upstream from Marsh Road. When the middle schooler, Lucas, dripped liquid across the counter, she let him practice with plain water until he could measure correctly on his own.

At the end of the week Dr. Osei offered all three interns a place for the rest of the summer. He asked Ines, though, to lead the program for student visitors. "Anyone can learn to be fast," he told her. "I need someone who finds the real cause and leaves the next person able to do the work."$q$, 'draft')
